  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A Four Corners-Upper Level Trough\n(4C/ULT) hybrid setup continues to reside over the western CONUS.\nThe western flank of the HPC to our east continues to pump \nsufficient moisture into eastern NV resulting in afternoon \nconvection mainly over White Pine and Elko Counties. Antecedent \ndrought conditions and dried out fuels are mitigating the effects \nof precipitation associated with convection this week. Lightning \nstrikes have been successful in igniting numerous fires across the\narea. Thunderstorms on Wednesday and Thursday will continue to \ncreate hazardous fire behavior.\n\nThe area receives a double shot of moisture on Friday and into the\nweekend as residual moisture from a tropical system off the CA \nCoast will filter into western NV. PW values will be anomalously \nhigh (1+ to 1.2 inches) on Friday. This has resulted in a marginal\nrisk of excessive rainfall over the area according to WPC. \nRainfall amounts Friday and Friday night will range 0.25-0.5 \ninches across northern NV and western NV. Said rainfall and \nresulting cloud cover will lead to a cooling trend into the \nweekend that will send temperatures a few degrees below normal \nfor late August.\n\nThe eastern side of the state is forecast to receive more\nmonsoonal moisture lending to the soggy forecast this weekend\nthough model disagreement exists in the placement of the HPC later\nthis week. A southerly placement could mitigate moisture over the\nregion as a more westerly flow in eastern NV could shut off\nmoisture availability. A more northerly placement closer to its\npositioning now could result in an underestimation of monsoon\nactivity currently in the forecast. Future model runs will need to\nbe scrutinized as time grows closer to Friday.\n\nBeyond the weekend the 4C/ULT hybrid setup looks to break down\nresulting in the potential for quieter weather as we head into\nSeptember. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continues for\nafternoon thunderstorm activity over eastern NV through Thursday.\nNBM Wx grids were heavily modified from 18Z on Wednesday to 03Z on\nThursday due to a complete lack of thunderstorm representation.\nThe NBM continues to do a very poor job of accounting for\nconvection in the Great Basin in general. A combination of SST and\nSerpISC was used to raise PoPs values to a sufficient level so\nthat thunder could be realized in the Wx Grids. Confidence\ncontinues to grow regarding increased precipitation Friday and\nSaturday as well as an associated cooling trend.",
